---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Principles | Amitoj Singh" description="How I think about decisions, knowledge, and autonomy: five principles and a way to connect Sikh practice with knowledge creation.">
  <!-- Hero Photo -->
  <section class="container-zen pt-12 pb-8">
    <div class="max-w-3xl">
      <img
        src="/images/photography-optimized/DSCF0807.webp"
        alt="Tennis court in autumn"
        class="w-full h-72 object-cover"
        style="object-position: center center;"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container-zen section-zen pt-8">
    <div class="max-w-2xl">
      <h1 class="mb-8 text-balance">Principles</h1>

      <div class="accent-line mb-8"></div>

      <p class="text-lg text-ink-light mb-6">
        I think clearly when I separate three questions: <strong>what matters</strong>, <strong>what’s true</strong>, and <strong>what I’m willing to do about it</strong>. I think most of the friction in my personal and professional life comes from confusing these questions and assuming their answers are clearer than they are.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container-zen section-zen border-t border-oat-300">
    <div class="max-w-2xl">
      <div class="space-y-12">

        <div>
          <h2 class="text-2xl mb-6">First: Values set direction. Evidence sets method.</h2>
          <p class="text-ink-light mb-4">
            “Should we?” is a question about values. More data cannot answer it.
          </p>
          <p class="text-ink-light mb-4">
            “Can we?” is a question about what can be done. Experiments, measurement, and repeated attempts <em>can</em> answer it — slowly and imperfectly.
          </p>
          <p class="text-ink-light mb-4">So I try to answer each question in the right way:</p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-ink-light space-y-2">
            <li><strong>Route "should" upward</strong> (to values, identity, responsibility).</li>
            <li><strong>Route "can" downward</strong> (to practice, prototypes, tests, and feedback).</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h2 class="text-2xl mb-6">From there: Capabilities are discovered, not declared.</h2>
          <p class="text-ink-light mb-4">
            I don't trust self-images&mdash;mine or anyone else's.
          </p>
          <p class="text-ink-light mb-4">
            "I can do X" is a <strong>scientific claim</strong>: it becomes real through repeated attempts under stated conditions, with honest accounting of failures.
          </p>
          <p class="text-ink-light">
            Constraints matter, and failure teaches. But I try to focus on <strong>building capability</strong>: expanding what I can reliably do, rather than only reducing what I avoid.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h2 class="text-2xl mb-6">Then: Codify what works.</h2>
          <p class="text-ink-light mb-4">
            When something proves true repeatedly, I turn it:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-ink-light mb-4 space-y-2">
            <li>into a <strong>principle</strong> when it requires judgment,</li>
            <li>into a <strong>system</strong> when it can be made deterministic, following fixed rules.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-ink-light">
            This makes knowledge useful in practice: fewer decisions require fresh willpower each time.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h2 class="text-2xl mb-6">But always: Protect what matters from what's measurable.</h2>
          <p class="text-ink-light mb-4">
            Metrics are useful, but they're not neutral.
          </p>
          <p class="text-ink-light">
            What’s visible tends to become “real,” and what’s “real” tends to govern our decisions. I try to keep slow commitments that cannot be measured — care, integrity, meaning — from being displaced by fast-moving measures that stand in for them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h2 class="text-2xl mb-6">Therefore: Earn autonomy. Don't gamble for it.</h2>
          <p class="text-ink-light">
            More freedom should come from demonstrated capability and tighter feedback loops, rather than from pretending risk is virtue.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container-zen section-zen border-t border-oat-300">
    <div class="max-w-2xl">
      <h2 class="text-2xl mb-8 section-header">A Sikh triad for knowledge creation</h2>

      <p class="text-ink-light mb-8">
        I connect three practices from Sikh tradition with how we create knowledge:
      </p>

      <div class="space-y-8">
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl mb-4 text-sage-700">Naam Japna &mdash; humility (honesty about what we know)</h3>
          <p class="text-ink-light mb-4">
            Humility is the beginning of science.
          </p>
          <p class="text-ink-light mb-4">
            It's the stance that says: <em>I might be wrong; reality gets a vote; my model is not the world.</em>
          </p>
          <p class="text-ink-light">
            Practically: keep a log, look for disconfirming evidence, update without drama.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl mb-4 text-sage-700">Kirat Karna &mdash; hard work (experimentation)</h3>
          <p class="text-ink-light mb-4">
            Knowledge is built, not wished into existence.
          </p>
          <p class="text-ink-light mb-4">
            Hard work means running the trials: building prototypes, practicing, repeating attempts, and measuring results.
          </p>
          <p class="text-ink-light">
            Practically: choose a method, do the work, repeat until the signal is real.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl mb-4 text-sage-700">Vandh Chakna &mdash; sharing (letting others test and use the result)</h3>
          <p class="text-ink-light mb-4">
            A result that can't be shared isn't stable knowledge&mdash;it's personal luck.
          </p>
          <p class="text-ink-light mb-4">
            Sharing makes claims available to others, who can test them, extend them, and build on them.
          </p>
          <p class="text-ink-light">
            Practically: publish what you made, teach the method, share the credit.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-oat-100 p-8 mt-8">
        <p class="text-xl text-center text-ink">
          <strong>humility &rarr; experiment &rarr; share &rarr; collective capability.</strong>
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="text-ink-light mt-8">
        Science, at its best, uses discovery to expand what we can reliably do together.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container-zen section-zen border-t border-oat-300 bg-oat-100">
    <div class="max-w-2xl">
      <h2 class="text-2xl mb-6">Continue</h2>

      <p class="text-ink-light mb-8">
        These principles inform the work I do with organizations and the writing I publish.
      </p>

      <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4">
        <a href="/writing" class="arrow-link">Read my writing</a>
        <a href="/about" class="arrow-link">About my work</a>
        <a href="/contact" class="arrow-link">Get in touch</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
